{
  "image_name": "5a1f6e9fe4536aa175f83b4070edfe6f.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how genetic information is packaged into chromatin and regulated by chemical marks on both DNA and histone proteins. At the top, DNA is shown wrapped around histone cores to form nucleosomes, which fold into chromatin and ultimately compact into a chromosome. Small colored dots indicate molecular modifications placed either directly on DNA or on histone tails.\n\nThe lower part emphasizes the dynamic “write, erase, read” system that controls these marks. Enzymes add and remove histone lysine methylation and acetylation, as well as DNA methylation on cytosine residues. Specifically, histone marks are handled by methyltransferases and demethylases, or acetyltransferases and deacetylases, while DNA methylation is added by DNMTs and removed by TET-related pathways. Reader proteins recognize these marks: MBD proteins bind methylated DNA, and several chromatin-binding domains recognize modified histones.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that chromatin state is not static. DNA methylation and histone modifications are coordinated across neighboring nucleosomes, and their recognition by reader proteins helps shape gene regulation by influencing how tightly or loosely DNA is packaged.",
